<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Han</h1><p class="st-meta">403 BCE – 230 BCE · capital Xinzheng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The smallest of the three states carved from Jin, home of the administrator Shen Buhai and the philosopher Han Fei, and the first state conquered by Qin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Han lineage, ministers of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-jin">State of Jin</a>, shared in the destruction of the Zhi clan in 453 BCE and the partition of Jin. In 403 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou</a> king recognised Marquis Jing as a ruler. In 375 BCE Han conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-zheng">State of Zheng</a> and moved its capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinzheng">Xinzheng</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Han held a central but exposed position on the routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> to the eastern plain. Under Marquis Zhao the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-buhai">Shen Buhai</a> developed methods for controlling officials, and Han's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbows</a> and swords were renowned. Its prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Fei</a> wrote the classic statement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legalism">Legalism</a>. Han's attempt to exhaust Qin by having the engineer <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-zheng">Zheng</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-state">Guo</a> build a canal backfired by enriching Qin.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Han was the first of the six states to fall: in 230 BCE the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> captured King An and made his land a commandery. The name Han was later revived for the kingdom granted to a royal descendant in 206 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-han.jpg" alt="State of Han" width="320" height="295"><figcaption>Image: Philg88 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EN-HAN260BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>403 BCE – 230 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xinzheng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Marquis Zhao of Han, King An of Han</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hann, Han (Warring States)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-316&amp;focus=state-of-han" aria-label="State of Han on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7571 931 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Han lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7891 1187L7768 1187M7891 1187L7789 1186"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1187h0M7789 1186h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7891 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7891 1187h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-316&amp;focus=state-of-han">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
